# dylo brand rules

dylo is a technology provider. We build and look after web apps for companies
with 1 to 500 employees and no tech team. The buyer is an owner or a director.
The name comes from the Czech word _dílo_, a piece of work. dylo has no
tagline. The visual system is warm monochrome, editorial in type and space,
with a single oxide note.

Full system: https://github.com/dyloAI/dylo-design · machine-readable index at
`/llms.txt` on the docs site.

## Non-negotiables

- **The brand is lowercase.** Always `dylo`. The work is capitalised: a Dílo.
- **One accent per view.** Oxide (`--dylo-oxide`, `#9A5236`) appears exactly
  once — the keystone, one link, or the single number that matters. An
  `<Eyebrow>` already spends it: its tick is the accent.
- **Never a second hue.** No green for success, no red for danger. Status is
  carried by the Badge's mono type and border weight.
- **Two families only.** Space Grotesk for anything human, Space Mono for the
  technical layer — eyebrows, labels, data, metadata — uppercase with wide
  tracking. Never a third.
- **No emoji. Ever.** Line icons only, 2px square-cut stroke, from Lucide.
- **No raw hex, ever.** Use the theme utilities: `bg-ink`, `text-oxide`,
  `border-line`, `shadow-block`. Outside Tailwind, `var(--dylo-*)`.
- **Two panels.** `Card` `variant="plate"` is the stamped plate: an ink border
  plus `shadow-block`. Reserve it for the primary action and for a product
  mock. `variant="editorial"` is the quiet panel: a hairline `border-line`,
  Paper, no shadow. Marketing copy uses editorial. The default stays `plate`
  so existing apps do not change.
- **Flat and opaque.** No gradients as decoration, no photographic backgrounds,
  no glass or blur. The only pattern is the 48px engineering grid
  (`grid-paper`). The one translucent touch is the oxide focus ring on fields.
- **Concrete is not text on Bone or Paper.** `#8E857A` on `#ECE6D8` is about
  2.9:1 and fails WCAG AA. Use it for rules, borders and disabled states.
  Secondary text is Graphite. On Ink, text is Bone.
- **Motion.** Interaction uses `--dylo-duration-fast` (150ms) and
  `--dylo-ease-out`. Hover darkens; a plate press shifts down and right into
  its shadow. A scroll reveal uses the `reveal` utility: opacity plus an 8px
  rise over `--dylo-duration-reveal` (400ms), and it does nothing when the
  user prefers reduced motion. No bounces, no float loops, and never fade an
  interactive element.
- **Never redraw the keystone mark.** Use the files in `public/brand/`. No
  rotating, recolouring, restacking or effects.

## Which layer to build on

**Product apps** (internal dylo apps, client apps, `dylo-templates/*`) install
`theme` + `catalyst-theme`. Auth screens and app shell live in **dylo-starter**
(Catalyst) — do not replace them with foundry `auth-kit` / `app-shell` from this
registry.

- Keep using Catalyst primitives. **Do not fork Catalyst's Button, Input or
  Badge to make them on-brand** — the theme already did that, and a fork will
  drift.
- `zinc-*` and `blue-*` utilities are correct there; they resolve to the dylo
  palette.
- Reach into the dylo registry only for what Catalyst lacks: `Eyebrow` and the
  foundry `Card`.

**Marketing / brand collateral** — sites, static mocks, proposals — install the
foundry dylo components and use them directly.

Install with the shadcn CLI, do not clone the repo:

```bash
pnpm dlx shadcn@latest add dyloAI/dylo-design/theme
pnpm dlx shadcn@latest list dyloAI/dylo-design
```

## Voice

Calm and trustworthy. Plain words. We say what it costs and when it will be
ready. Honest, short, and free of hype.

Call dylo a technology provider. In anything a customer reads, do not call it
an agency, a consultancy, or a software factory, and do not mention AI.

### How we write

- Plain and reassuring. Short sentences. Say the thing, then stop.
- Honest. If a date is not known yet, say so. Do not imply a promise we cannot
  keep.
- Clear about cost and time. A price and a date belong in the first
  explanation.
- Numbers over adjectives. "A working web app in six weeks" is the form to use.
- First and second person: "we" (dylo) and "you" (the client).
- The brand is lowercase: `dylo`. The work is capitalised: a Dílo.
- No emoji. Ever.
- No buzzwords: leverage, synergy, best-in-class, cutting-edge, seamless,
  unlock, supercharge.

### No tagline

dylo has no tagline. Do not write "Software, made.", "Software, hecho.", or a
line under the logo. The promise is the one-line pitch: a one-off fee, then
from €30 a month. The footer carries the legal line: dylo is a brand of Ax42
S.L.

### Messaging

Customer-facing words are the messaging framework, rendered at
`/foundations/messaging` (source: `src/lib/messaging.ts`). Use that file.
Do not invent a price, a client, or a competitor to fill a TODO.

Positioning: dylo is a technology provider for companies with 1 to 500 people
and no technology team. You and a dylo engineer define the system on the dylo
platform. We start with a narrow product we already build: a quote or product
configurator, data extraction and analysis, or an operations platform. A
one-off fee pays for the build. When it is live, we host it and look after
it from €30 a month. A more complex app, such as a 3D configurator, sits
toward a few hundred euros a month. Do not name the client. The customer can
take the app elsewhere whenever they want. We hand it over.

One-line pitch: "A dylo engineer builds your web app for a one-off fee, and
we look after it from €30 a month." Spanish: "Un ingeniero de dylo construye
su aplicación web por un pago único, y la mantenemos desde 30 € al mes."

Pillars: a dylo engineer on the platform; a product we already know; a price
and a date first; one monthly fee.

dylo is a brand of Ax42 S.L.

### Tone by channel

**Website.** The one-line pitch, then who it is for, that we build the app and
look after it, and that the price and the date come first. No tagline.

**Proposals and quotes.** Name the app, the price, what is included, and the
date. Say what looking after it costs after launch. Every claim that can be a
number should be a number.

**Email.** Write as you would to a director you have already met. One subject,
one ask. If you are waiting on them, say what you need and by when.

**Social.** One fact: a price range, a timeline, or who it is for. No emoji.
Do not add a tagline under the logo.

### English

Do: "We will build the web app in six weeks. The price is in the quote, and we
look after it once it is live."

Don't: "We leverage synergies to deliver best-in-class digital transformation."

Do: "You do not need a tech team. We build the app, then we keep it running."

Don't: "Supercharge your workflow with a seamless AI-native platform."

### Spanish

Same rules: plain, short, a price and a date. Use "usted" with an owner or
director unless they have already used "tú". The brand stays lowercase: dylo.
There is no Spanish tagline. The pitch uses usted: "Un ingeniero de dylo
construye su aplicación web por un pago único, y la mantenemos desde 30 € al
mes."

Do: "Le construimos la aplicación web en seis semanas. El precio está en el
presupuesto, y la mantenemos cuando ya está en uso."

Don't: "Potenciamos sinergias para una transformación digital de primer nivel."

Do: "No necesita un equipo técnico. La construimos y la mantenemos."

Don't: "Impulse su negocio con una plataforma innovadora de última generación."
